<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Otto II, Holy Roman Emperor</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/955">955</a>–<a href="/years/983">983</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottonian-dynasty">Ottonian dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Otto II was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-emperor">Holy Roman Emperor</a> from 973, son of Otto I and husband of the Byzantine princess Theophanu. In 976 he gave the march on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> to Leopold of Babenberg, the beginning of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>. He fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danes">Danes</a> and the French and was defeated by the Saracens at Stilo in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> in 982, dying in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> the next year.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/otto-ii-holy-roman-emperor.jpg" alt="Otto II, Holy Roman Emperor" width="320" height="445" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Meister des Registrum Gregorii · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Otto_II_(HRE).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 980 <b>Otto II</b> issued a diploma confirming the immunity of the church of Liège, removing its lands from the jurisdiction of royal counts, and in 985 Otto III granted Notger the county of Huy, the first of many counties acquired by the see.</blockquote><a href="/empire/prince-bishopric-of-liege">Prince-Bishopric of Liège · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 976 <b>Emperor Otto II</b> separated Carinthia from the Duchy of Bavaria to weaken the rebellious Duke Henry the Quarrelsome, creating the first new duchy of the Empire.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-carinthia">Duchy of Carinthia</a></li><li><blockquote>The abbesses Mathilde, a granddaughter of Otto I, and Theophanu, granddaughter of <b>Otto II</b>, made Essen a centre of Ottonian art; the Golden Madonna of about 980 and the Otto-Mathilde Cross belong to its treasury.</blockquote><a href="/state/imperial-abbey-of-essen">Imperial Abbey of Essen</a></li><li><blockquote>In 976 <b>Otto II</b> gave it to Leopold of Babenberg.</blockquote><a href="/state/margraviate-of-austria">Margraviate of Austria</a></li><li><blockquote>In 982 <b>Emperor Otto II</b> granted the bishop extensive rights over the city, and later Ottonian and Salian grants added counties and estates in Alsace and the Ortenau, making the bishops princes of the Holy Roman Empire.</blockquote><a href="/state/prince-bishopric-of-strasbourg">Prince-Bishopric of Strasbourg</a></li></ul></section>
